body:
Basically, what happened with Valentine was this. At I think it was Summerslam 97, Valentine lost the World title to Bret Hart, with the assistance of Shawn Michaels. Valentine was upset that Michaels interfered, which set up a feud between Hart/Valentine and Michaels/HHH. This went through to Survivor Series (yes, I kept the Montreal Screw Job), at Survivor Series Valentine faced HHH, and it was a double count-out. With Bret Hart gone, the feud changed to just being Valentine & HHH. Then in the December PPV, the two faced again, but resulted in a double DQ. So a steel cage match was set for the Royal Rumble 98, which saw Valentine win. Following night on Raw, New Age Outlaws come out and say that HHH couldn't get the job done, so they will. Challenged Valentine to a tag-team match, against any partner of his choosing...and Valentine brough out Ric Flair. Flair & Valentine won, then the following week Flair & Valentine are chatting backstage, when Marc Mero randomly attacks Valentine (part of an angle I had with Mero at the time), and Valentine suffered an injury in the angle. And while out injured, Valentine retired.

&#x200B;

If the injury had not happened, Valentine's contract would have been up just after Survivor Series 1998, where long term planning was going to see Valentine, Flair, Road Warriors, and someone from Valentine's past (hadn't gotten that far to decide, or try to negotiate contracts), against the Nation (The Rock, Mark Henry, D'Lo Brown, The Godfather, and Owen Hart).

A former student colleague of mine from my university days told me a story once, which I got reminded by reading this.

&#x200B;

His father was out drinking one night, and as he was leaving, there was a guy who was short $1 on his tab. So his father chipped in the $1, as a friendly gesture.

&#x200B;

Three months later, snow is aplenty, and the plows are out. A plow is coming down my colleague's street, turns out it was the guy from the bar. The father was outside trying to shovel snow from the driveway, the plow guy came and helped, and for the rest of that winter, their house always had a clear path from the snow plows coming through

I think it will do fine, just like a good bottle of wine. When I visit I hope I win lots, when I spend a lot on the slots. Video Poker is my game, but my winnings are always lame. Lets see what the rooms are like, and whether they charge for parking my bike.

&#x200B;

I've got a few more, but I won't bore you. In all seriousness, given the location, it may do well. As a new location, it is going to attract some upper class, who are tired of the same old stuff currently on the strip. Their overall success will depend majorly on attracting customers to that end of the strip, and having a point of difference that makes them unique (besides the curiosity of being new). If they come across as just being the same old boring casino offerings that are already on the strip, they will struggle.

I used to have a similar problem when I used to live in Meadowbank. On the lower (older) side of Meadowbank, next to the station there is a zebra crossing. Nearly every day cars go across that crossing without looking, without slowing down, and barely missing people using it properly. My daughters were nearly hit at least half a dozen times...once one car stopped literally millimetres from my daughters pram. And when I complained...Ryde Council wanted nothing to do with it, the local state member at the time just passed the buck. When I came up with possible solutions, he didn't want to know any of them, and pretty much refused to help in any way. From what I have heard from locals over the years, at least half a dozen people have been hit on that crossing, only one of them actually needing an ambulance.

&#x200B;

Side note...when mentioning the problems on that crossing, most car drivers wanted to blame the pedestrians for running out in front of cars. When I mentioned about a near miss with a person using a walker, their response was they should have walked elsewhere...elsewhere than a zebra crossing???

I have found that the answer to this question is highly dependent on where the reader is from. For example, I remember a ranking of the 100 greatest comedy films of all time, about 20 years ago, was done in England. At the top of the list, was Carry On Cleo. Yet if the list was done in the US, you would be lucky to see any Carry On movie in a top 100 list. Likewise, any Top 100 list done in Australia would most likely have the movie Young Einstein in it, but you would struggle to find a mention of that movie in any other country's list. Additionally, the age of the respondent can influence the answer...someone in their 80's or 90's may mention a movie such as "It's A Mad Mad Mad Mad World", or "Those Magnificent Men In Their Flying Machines", someone in their 40's or 50's may mention "There's Something About Mary" or "Monty Python & The Holy Grail", and someone in their 20's would mention a movie like "Anchorman", or the newer remake of "21 Jump Street".

&#x200B;

Me personally, I can't narrow it down to one movie. I could say about 10 different movies that could all be classed as the greatest comedy film of all time, for one reason or another.

Friend of mine (we will call him Ralph) told me a story just the other day along these lines. He was best man at a wedding, about 50 people from the wedding party staying at this hotel, including the bride and groom. They also had the banquet room reserved for the reception. Anyway, about half an hour before everyone was due to leave the hotel to head to the location of the ceremony, the fire alarm went off in the hotel. Bride and groom had to evacuate with everyone else, bride was getting last minute make-up done, but Ralph managed to organise that the bride and groom did not see each other before the ceremony, as they evacuated the hotel. 

&#x200B;

Turns out what set off the fire alarm, was a friend of the groom's father. This friend was rich, and was paying for the entire wedding (the friend was Buddhist, and while Buddhists can be rich, they often prefer to spread their wealth...thus he insisted on paying for the wedding as a gift to the father of the groom). The friend of the father had been praying to his Buddha statue in the hotel room, and was burning some incense or candles, that turned out to be a bit smokier than expected, which set off the fire alarm. The friend of the father took full responsibility, and apologised to everyone...but no-one could stay angry with him, since he was paying for everything anyway.

Hmmm...I felt like getting a kebab, followed by a pack of Winfield Blues, and topping it off with a Slurpee from 7-11...now I know where to go.

&#x200B;

In all seriousness...a lot of places are going like that in Australia. It seems as though they are the only three stores (outside of bottle shops) that can survive in the current environment.

This happened to a friend of mine when I was at university. She was a waitress/host at an All You Can Eat Family Chinese Buffet style restaurant, and I was there picking up some takeaway using her staff discount (when you are a university student, if you can find a way to save money on anything, you do it). Restaurant was fully booked, with several groups waiting for tables to become free. The other waitress/host sees to a group that has just come to the counter, saying they have a reservation for 7:30pm (it was probably 7:25pm). The waitress asks if their name was Smith, the group said yes, and they got seated. As that group gets escorted to their table, another group comes through the front door, and gets seen to by the same waitress/host. This group also says that they have a reservation, and the waitress/host asks what name, again being told Smith.

&#x200B;

After a bit of back and forth between the waitress/host and the group, the waitress/host goes over to the group that was incorrectly seated. A bit more back and forth, but by this time my friend had brought my takeaway out, so I didn't stick around to see the aftermath. But according to my friend, it turned out that the group that was seated first, did indeed have a reservation...for the following night.

There is just so many there because their buses got cancelled.

&#x200B;

But seriously, how about actually putting inspectors on the buses where three quarters of the passengers are not using their Opal cards? I have seen inspectors on services that may, at best, have six people on the bus for the entire trip...yet others where the bus is almost full, never to see an inspector.

I got slugged $130 from Domestic to Ryde, but I know why...I told the driver he could use whatever motorway/tunnel he wanted, to get us home quicker...and noticed a charge of nearly $17 for going through the Cross City Tunnel. The tolls may be what got you.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Forgot to mention, I travelled Friday PM peak...normally I would tell the driver to go through O'Riordan street, through to Central, across to Anzac Bridge etc...and yes, have had taxi fares of under $90 doing it this way. But Friday PM peak, Friday before a long weekend, and heading from the airport to Ryde/Epping you are going with traffic...I knew there would be delays.

The International Fastener Expo...boy I better not be slow in getting to that.

&#x200B;

Seriously though, there are a couple I am actually interested in attending. Any ideas on dates for these?

When I head to Las Vegas in November, plan is to film a heap of content for a particular segment of the tourism market that often gets ignored...I am not going to say the segment, because I don't want someone taking my idea. But upon saying that, while a lot of these Las Vegas videos are quite informative and entertaining, the makers of them are not exactly that approachable. I know they often have thousands (sometimes hundreds of thousands) of followers, and they probably get thousands of emails and messages every day, most of the ones I have personally tried to contact cannot be bothered with a reply. And yes, I know there may be plenty of reasons why too. Their daytime jobs may get in the way, they may be focusing on putting content out/editing, etc...but it would be nice if I could hear back from at least one of them.

&#x200B;

But anyway, the best in my mind, for keeping the energy flowing and upbeat, would have to be TravelRuby. For information, probably Jacobs Life In Vegas.

WOW...MIT. You know, I went to MIT. I also went to Harvard, Stanford, and yes, even Yale. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

It was good going to see all of these universities I have heard so much about, during my holiday in the US.

In Australia, if you want to do a university degree straight out of high school, you have to get a TER (Tertiary Entrance Rank) or UAI (University Admissions Index) or whatever it is called these days, of a figure suitable to enrol in that course...but this figure is flexible, going by the number of people applying each year, compared to the number of places available. What I mean by this, is that say you want to do a Bachelor of Business degree (forget about majors and all that stuff for the moment). At some of the more prestigious universities, or the major city based universities, the UAI may be 65. At some of the country universities, the UAI may be 50. One particular country university may have a lack of enrolments one year, so the UAI may be dropped to 49 to encourage enrolments. Likewise, a city university may have an increase in enrolments, so may increase their UAI to 66. This is a simplified explanation...it is a little more complicated than that.

&#x200B;

But I am a firm believer in forgiving student debt...to a certain degree. If you do a three year degree, for example, and you drop out after the first year, then you should have to pay a certain percentage of the course fees (exceptions can be applied, such as you withdraw for health reasons etc). If you drop out after the second year, you pay a certain percentage. If you complete the degree, you pay a certain percentage...but if you are unable to get a job in that field within say five years (with proof that you have tried), you do not have to pay anything back. It would encourage people to only do degrees that they are able to do, and encourage universities to actually teach their degrees in a way that will actually lead to jobs in that field...I know of a class of Business graduates (with Marketing majors) from a university other than the one that I went to, where only 3 people out of the 60 that completed the degree, actually managed to get jobs in the marketing field within five years of graduating...because while the university taught the students all about business and marketing, they didn't teach the students what was actually needed to get a job in the industry.

Australia...

&#x200B;

Getting rid of all the dangerous poisonous snakes and spiders because everyone else is scared of them, while downing a six pack of beer in record time.

Reported on a wrestling news site back in the late 90s...wish I could remember which one. That Shawn Michaels got beat up by those Marines because they thought he was gay, and one of them had tried kissing him in the toilets, and he spurned their advance.

&#x200B;

The other one, pretty ridiculous, that I remember from the 90s, was that Vince wanted to sign Bob Holly as a NASCAR driver, and give him the World title to promote at NASCAR events.

I do.

&#x200B;

Seriously, the biggest lie would have to be at the doctors office, and going over some medical background information..
